  ## Code review findings
  
  **Thoroughness: high** (~1400 LoC across 12 source files, all read in full.)
  
  Proc-macro implementation crate for the color-print library. Provides macros
  (cformat!, cprint!, cprintln!, cstr!, untagged!, etc.) that parse color tags
  in format strings and replace them with ANSI escape sequences at compile time.
  
  ### Non-negotiable checks
  
  - **build.rs**: None (explicitly `build = false`). Positive.
  - **Dependencies**: syn, quote, proc-macro2 (standard proc-macro ecosystem),
    nom (parser combinators for tag syntax). All well-known. Positive.
  - **FFI**: None. Positive.
  - **`unsafe`**: Zero. Positive.
  
  ### Red-flag scan
  
  Clean. No network access, no filesystem I/O, no environment variable reads,
  no process spawning, no include_bytes!/include_str!, no obfuscated strings.
  Pure compile-time string transformation only.
  
  ### Architecture
  
  - lib.rs: 9 proc-macro entry points, all thin wrappers around get_macro()
  - ansi.rs: ANSI code path — transforms color tags into escape sequences
  - terminfo.rs: terminfo code path — uses named arguments referencing runtime constants
  - color_context.rs: Tracks open/close tag state, computes state diffs for minimal ANSI output
  - parse/color_tag.rs: nom-based parser for color tag syntax (<red>, <bold>, <rgb(1,2,3)>, etc.)
  - format_args/: Parses format!-like arguments, splits format string into Text/Placeholder/ColorTag nodes
  - untagged.rs: Strips color tags entirely (for untagged! macro)
  
  Two feature-gated code paths: default (hardcoded ANSI) and terminfo (runtime constants).
  Auto-closes unclosed tags at end of format string. Good error messages via syn::Error.
  
  ## Rating rationale
  
  Positive: Well-structured proc-macro crate that does exactly what it claims. Zero unsafe,
  no build script, no I/O of any kind. Standard proc-macro dependencies plus nom for parsing.
  Source verified against upstream. Good test coverage for tag parsing.
