comment: |-
  # Review: lockable 0.1.1
  
  - Local source: /home/dpc/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/lockable-0.1.1
  - Upstream repository: https://github.com/smessmer/lockable
  - Upstream commit verified: 814e2ea130721ee4875c85ca8a7f93b080938684
  - Upstream tag for version: 0.1.1
  - Verification outcome: pass
  
  ## Claims baseline
  
  The crate claims to provide thread-safe HashMap, LruCache, and LockPool data structures
  where individual entries can be locked/unlocked by key, useful for synchronizing access
  to an underlying key-value store or building cache data structures on top of one.
  
  ## External verification
  
  Package matches public repo https://github.com/smessmer/lockable at commit
  814e2ea130721ee4875c85ca8a7f93b080938684, which is the commit tagged as `0.1.1`.
  `diff -r --brief` (excluding expected files) produced zero differences. All files
  are bit-for-bit identical between the crates.io tarball and the upstream repository
  at the verified commit.
  
  ## Code review findings
  
  Thoroughness level: low (crate has ~4800 non-test Rust LoC, in the 1500-10000 range).
  
  ### Non-negotiable checks
  
  - **build.rs**: No custom build script. Positive.
  - **Cargo.toml dependencies**: `futures`, `lru` (optional), `tokio` (sync, time),
    `derive_more` (display, error), `itertools`. All are consistent with the crate's
    stated purpose (async concurrency data structures). No suspicious dependencies.
  - **proc-macro**: Not a proc-macro crate.
  - **unsafe**: `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` declared in lib.rs (line 107). Grep for `unsafe`
    confirms only that `forbid` directive exists. No unsafe code whatsoever. Positive.
  - **FFI surface**: None.
  
  ### Per-file read status
  
  - `src/lib.rs` (134 lines) - **read in full**. Module declarations, re-exports, `forbid(unsafe_code)`, `deny(missing_docs)`.
  - `src/guard.rs` (413 lines) - **read in full**. RAII guard struct with key/value access, insert/remove/try_insert/value_or_insert_with operations. Drop impl calls `_unlock`. Clean, well-documented with doc-tests.
  - `src/limit.rs` (301 lines) - **read in full**. `AsyncLimit` and `SyncLimit` enums with `NoLimit` and `SoftLimit` variants. Eviction callback pattern is well-documented, including infinite loop warning.
  - `src/lockable_map_impl.rs` (766 lines) - **read in full**. Core implementation. Two-tier locking: `std::sync::Mutex` for the map, `tokio::sync::Mutex` per entry. Well-documented invariants (2A, 2B, 2C) for Arc reference counting correctness. The `_unlock` method and cleanup logic follow the invariants carefully. `EntriesGuard` wrapper enforces invariants in debug/test mode.
  - `src/lockable_hash_map.rs` (874 lines) - **read in full (first 200 lines)**; **read partially (remaining lines, red-flag scan only)**. HashMap-backed implementation of the `MapLike` trait and `LockableHashMap` struct with all lock variants (blocking, async, try, owned).
  - `src/lockable_lru_cache.rs` (1540 lines) - **not read** (LRU feature variant; similar pattern to hash map).
  - `src/lockable_trait.rs` (78 lines) - **read in full**. Trait definition with associated types for guards and limits.
  - `src/lockpool.rs` (411 lines) - **read in full**. Wrapper around `LockableHashMap<K, ()>` with simplified API. Several TODO comments for unimplemented owned variants.
  - `src/map_like.rs` (32 lines) - **read in full**. Trait abstraction for different map backends.
  - `src/utils/primary_arc.rs` (110 lines) - **read in full**. `PrimaryArc`/`ReplicaArc` pattern to control Arc cloning. Interesting design to enforce that only the primary can clone. One TODO note about `try_lock_owned` temporarily violating invariant via `Arc::clone` (line 77-78), but the note says call sites compensate.
  - `src/utils/stream.rs` (99 lines) - **read in full**. `next_if_ready` stream extension using noop waker.
  - `src/utils/time.rs` (50 lines) - **read in full**. Simple `TimeProvider` trait with real and mock implementations.
  - `src/utils/never.rs` (48 lines) - **read in full**. `Never` type (empty enum) and `InfallibleUnwrap` trait.
  - `src/utils/mod.rs` (8 lines) - **read in full**. Module declarations.
  - `src/tests.rs` (2795 lines) - **not read** (test code).
  - `benches/` - **not read** (benchmark code).
  
  ### Red-flag scan results
  
  - No `unsafe`, `transmute`, `from_raw`, raw pointers.
  - No `std::process::Command`, shell invocation, env var access.
  - No network libraries, `include_bytes!`, `include_str!`.
  - No obfuscated strings, hex blobs, or suspicious constants.
  - No `Drop` impls with side effects beyond the expected lock cleanup in `Guard::drop`.
  - `unwrap`/`expect` usage in non-test code: present in `_entries()` (mutex poisoning),
    `_guard()`, `_guard_mut()`, `Guard::drop`, and invariant assertions. All are on internal
    invariants that should hold by construction, not on untrusted input. Acceptable.
  
  ### Architecture summary
  
  The crate uses a two-tier locking scheme: a single `std::sync::Mutex` protects the
  overall map structure, while each entry has its own `tokio::sync::Mutex` for per-key
  locking across async boundaries. The `PrimaryArc`/`ReplicaArc` pattern restricts
  Arc cloning to enforce reference counting invariants. A `SoftLimit` eviction mechanism
  allows bounded caches with user-controlled eviction callbacks. The design is well-thought-out
  with carefully documented invariants.
  
  ### Claims vs reality reconciliation
  
  The crate does exactly what it claims: provides lockable HashMap, LRU cache, and lock pool
  data structures. No network access, filesystem access, process spawning, or env var reading.
  Dependencies are justified by the async concurrency use case.
  
  ## Cross-check against existing reviews
  
  No prior reviews of this crate at any version.
  
  ## Open questions / things skipped
  
  - `src/lockable_lru_cache.rs` (1540 lines) was not read; it follows the same pattern as
    the hash map implementation but with LRU ordering. At `low` thoroughness this is acceptable.
  - `src/tests.rs` and `benches/` were not read.
  - The TODO at `primary_arc.rs:77` about `try_lock_owned` temporarily violating the invariant
    via `Arc::clone` deserves deeper analysis at `medium` thoroughness but is noted by the author
    as currently safe due to call-site cleanup.
  
  ## Draft review fields
  
  - rating: positive
  - thoroughness: low
  - understanding: medium
