comment: |-
  # Review: darling_macro 0.20.11
  
  - Local source: /home/dpc/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/darling_macro-0.20.11
  - Upstream repository: https://github.com/TedDriggs/darling
  - Upstream commit verified: 82a51e0b65b158de02ffb5d753ea4cd03529743b
  - Upstream tag for version: v0.20.11
  - Verification outcome: pass
  
  ## External verification
  
  Package matches public repo https://github.com/TedDriggs/darling at commit
  `82a51e0b65b158de02ffb5d753ea4cd03529743b`, which is the commit tagged as
  `v0.20.11` corresponding to version 0.20.11. The sha from `.cargo_vcs_info.json`
  matches the tag exactly. All files under `macro/` in the upstream repo are
  bit-for-bit identical to the crates.io tarball (excluding expected cargo-added
  files: Cargo.toml, Cargo.toml.orig, .cargo_vcs_info.json, Cargo.lock).
  
  ## Code review findings
  
  Thoroughness level: **medium** (33 Rust LoC, small enough for careful end-to-end read).
  
  ### Claims baseline
  
  The crate describes itself as "Internal support for a proc-macro library for reading
  attributes into structs when implementing custom derives." It is the proc-macro
  entry-point crate for the `darling` ecosystem, providing derive macros (`FromMeta`,
  `FromDeriveInput`, `FromAttributes`, `FromField`, `FromTypeParam`, `FromVariant`)
  that delegate all real logic to `darling_core`.
  
  ### File map
  
  - `src/lib.rs` — **read in full**. 40 lines total. This is the only Rust source file.
  - `Cargo.toml.orig` — **read in full**. Manifest with 3 dependencies.
  - `LICENSE` — present (MIT).
  
  ### Non-negotiable checks
  
  - **`build.rs`**: Not present. Positive.
  - **Proc-macro**: Yes, `proc-macro = true` in Cargo.toml. All 7 `#[proc_macro_derive]`
    functions reviewed. Each one is a trivial delegation:
    1. `derive_from_meta` — parses input via `syn::parse_macro_input!`, delegates to
       `darling_core::derive::from_meta`, converts result to `TokenStream`.
    2. `derive_from_meta_item` — **deprecated stub**: does not parse input at all, just
       emits a compile error telling users the trait was renamed. The `_input` parameter
       is intentionally unused. No processing of attacker-controlled input.
    3. `derive_from_attributes` — same delegation pattern as #1, to `derive::from_attributes`.
    4. `derive_from_input` — delegates to `derive::from_derive_input`.
    5. `derive_field` — delegates to `derive::from_field`.
    6. `derive_type_param` — delegates to `derive::from_type_param`.
    7. `derive_variant` — delegates to `derive::from_variant`.
    All real logic lives in `darling_core`; this crate is purely a proc-macro entry-point shim.
  - **`unsafe`**: None. `rg -n unsafe` returns no hits.
  - **FFI surface**: None.
  - **Dependencies**: `quote 1.x`, `syn 2.x`, `darling_core =0.20.11`. All expected for a
    proc-macro derive crate. No surprising or unrelated dependencies.
  
  ### Red-flag scan
  
  - No `unsafe`, `transmute`, `from_raw`, raw pointers.
  - No `std::process::Command`, shell invocation.
  - No `std::env::var`, env var access.
  - No network libraries (`reqwest`, `ureq`, etc.).
  - No `include_bytes!`, `include_str!`.
  - No base64/hex blobs, obfuscated strings.
  - No `unwrap`/`expect`/`panic!` (only `parse_macro_input!` which produces compile errors on failure).
  - No conditional compilation.
  - No `Drop` impls.
  
  ### Architecture summary
  
  This crate is a thin proc-macro entry-point. It exists solely because Rust requires
  proc-macro crates to be separate from the logic crate. Each derive macro parses the
  input token stream using `syn`, hands it to the corresponding `darling_core::derive::*`
  function, and converts the resulting `proc_macro2::TokenStream` back to
  `proc_macro::TokenStream`. The `FromMetaItem` derive is a deprecated compatibility
  stub that emits a compile error directing users to `FromMeta`. The crate does nothing
  beyond delegation — all complexity, parsing, and code generation lives in `darling_core`.
  
  ### Claims vs reality reconciliation
  
  The crate claims to be internal proc-macro support for darling. That is exactly what
  the code does — 7 derive macros, all delegating to `darling_core`, no extra capabilities.
  No network, filesystem, process, env, FFI, or unsafe usage. Fully consistent with claims.
  
  ## Cross-check against existing reviews
  
  One prior review exists: version 0.9.0 reviewed by dpc (FYlr8YoYGVvDwHQxqEIs89reKKDy-oWisoO0qXXEfHE)
  on 2019-10-11, rated `positive` at `low` thoroughness and `low` understanding.
  
  The prior review has no comment text, so there are no specific findings to compare against.
  The `positive` rating is consistent with my findings — the crate is a straightforward,
  safe proc-macro shim. The code structure has evolved slightly since 0.9.0 (added
  `FromAttributes` derive, deprecated `FromMetaItem`), but the fundamental pattern is
  identical: thin delegation to `darling_core`.
  
  Verdict: existing review does not change my proposed fields. My `medium` thoroughness is
  justified by the complete end-to-end read of all code in this very small crate.
  
  ## Open questions / things skipped
  
  - The `Cargo.lock` file was not reviewed (it's a lockfile for the proc-macro crate,
    not shipped to downstream users).
  - The actual logic in `darling_core` is out of scope for this review — that would be
    a separate crev review of `darling_core 0.20.11`.
  
  ## Draft review fields
  
  - rating: positive
  - thoroughness: medium
  - understanding: medium
