comment: |-
  # Review: id-arena 2.3.0
  
  - Local source: /home/dpc/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/id-arena-2.3.0
  - Upstream repository: https://github.com/fitzgen/id-arena
  - Upstream commit verified: 52bd3681cf295a220f78de945d32a26ead712385
  - Upstream tag for version: no tag for 2.3.0 (latest tag is 2.2.1); commit message reads "Bump to version 2.3.0"
  - Verification outcome: pass
  
  ## External verification
  
  Package matches public repo https://github.com/fitzgen/id-arena at commit
  `52bd3681cf295a220f78de945d32a26ead712385`. The sha was taken from
  `.cargo_vcs_info.json` and the commit exists upstream with commit message
  "Bump to version 2.3.0". No version tag exists for 2.3.0 (the most recent
  tag is `2.2.1`), but this is a minor finding — the author may have simply
  stopped tagging releases. The `Cargo.toml.orig` is bit-for-bit identical
  to the upstream `Cargo.toml`. All other files are bit-for-bit identical.
  No discrepancies found.
  
  ## Claims baseline
  
  The crate claims to be "a simple, id-based arena": allocate objects, get
  an integer-based `Id` back (not a reference), and use that id to index
  into the arena. No deletion. Homogeneous. Supports `no_std` (with alloc)
  and optional `rayon` parallel iteration. No I/O, no network, no FFI, no
  unsafe code.
  
  ## Code review findings
  
  Thoroughness level: **medium** — the crate is ~1000 lines across 3 source
  files, small enough to read end-to-end carefully.
  
  ### Non-negotiable checks
  
  - **`build.rs`**: None present. Positive.
  - **`#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`**: Present at `src/lib.rs:101`. The entire
    crate forbids unsafe code at the compiler level. No `unsafe` blocks
    exist anywhere. Positive.
  - **Proc-macro**: Not a proc-macro crate. Positive.
  - **FFI surface**: None. No `extern "C"`, no `bindgen`, no linked C
    libraries. Positive.
  - **Dependencies**: Only `rayon` (optional). Appropriate for a crate that
    advertises optional parallel iteration support. No surprising deps.
  
  ### Per-file review
  
  - **`src/lib.rs` (727 lines)** — **read in full**.
    - `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`, `#![no_std]` with conditional `alloc`/`std`
      extern crate. Clean.
    - `ArenaBehavior` trait: defines how ids map to indices. Uses a global
      `AtomicUsize` counter (`ARENA_COUNTER`) with `SeqCst` ordering to
      generate unique arena ids. The counter is cast to `u32` which will
      silently wrap after 2^32 arenas — a theoretical collision risk but
      practically irrelevant and documented implicitly by the `u32` type.
    - `Id<T>` struct: `usize` index + `u32` arena_id + `PhantomData`.
      Manually implements `Copy`, `Clone`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `Hash`,
      `PartialOrd`, `Ord`, `Debug`. All implementations are straightforward
      and correct.
    - `Arena<T, A>` struct: wraps a `Vec<T>` with an arena_id. Methods:
      `new`, `with_capacity`, `alloc`, `alloc_with_id`, `next_id`, `get`,
      `get_mut`, `iter`, `iter_mut`, `len`. All are thin wrappers around
      `Vec` operations with arena_id checks. Clean.
    - `Index` and `IndexMut` impls: use `assert_eq!` to panic on wrong
      arena_id, then index into the vec. This is documented behavior (panics
      on wrong arena).
    - Iterator types (`Iter`, `IterMut`, `IntoIter`): all delegate to
      `Enumerate<slice::Iter/IterMut>` or `Enumerate<vec::IntoIter>`,
      wrapping index+item pairs into `(Id, &T)` tuples via the `add_id`
      helper. All implement `Iterator`, `DoubleEndedIterator`, and
      `ExactSizeIterator`. Straightforward.
    - `alloc_with_id` has a subtle double-allocation pattern: it calls
      `self.next_id()` then `self.alloc(val)`, and `alloc` calls
      `self.next_id()` again internally. The second call to `next_id` will
      return a *different* id (index is now len+1 after push), but `alloc`
      returns the id from the second `next_id` call — which has index
      equal to the length *before* the push (since push happens after
      `next_id` in `alloc`). Wait, re-reading: `alloc` calls `next_id`
      (which returns `A::new_id(arena_id, self.items.len())`) then pushes.
      So in `alloc_with_id`: first `next_id` returns id with index=len,
      then `self.alloc(val)` calls `next_id` again (still index=len, since
      nothing was pushed yet), pushes, returns the id. So the returned id
      from `alloc` matches the id passed to `f`. This is correct.
    - Test module: single test checking `Id<Foo>` is `Send + Sync`. Fine.
  
  - **`src/rayon.rs` (283 lines)** — **read in full**.
    - Implements `ParallelIterator` and `IndexedParallelIterator` for
      `ParIter`, `ParIterMut`, and `IntoParIter`. All delegate to rayon's
      own implementations on the underlying slice/vec iterators, wrapping
      with `add_id`-style closures. Straightforward, no surprises.
    - `IntoParallelIterator` impls for `&Arena`, `&mut Arena`, and `Arena`.
      All correct.
  
  - **`tests/readme_up_to_date.rs` (22 lines)** — **read in full**.
    - Test that runs `cargo readme` and compares output to `README.md`.
      Uses `std::process::Command` to shell out to `cargo readme`. This is
      a test-only file and does not ship in the library. No concern.
  
  ### Red-flag scan results
  
  - `unsafe`: zero hits (and `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` enforces this).
  - `transmute`, `from_raw`, raw pointers: none.
  - `Command::new`, `std::env::var`: only in `tests/readme_up_to_date.rs` (test file, not library code).
  - Network libraries (`reqwest`, `hyper`, etc.): none.
  - `include_bytes!`, `include_str!`: none.
  - Base64/hex blobs, obfuscated strings: none.
  - `unwrap`/`expect`/`panic!`: `assert_eq!` in `Index`/`IndexMut` impls (expected, panics on wrong arena id). `expect` in test file only.
  
  ### Claims vs reality reconciliation
  
  The crate claims to be a simple id-based arena with no_std support and
  optional rayon. The code does exactly this and nothing more. No I/O, no
  network, no filesystem, no env vars, no FFI, no unsafe. The only
  dependency is `rayon` (optional), which is justified. All capabilities
  match the claims.
  
  ## Cross-check against existing reviews
  
  No prior reviews of this crate at any version in the local proof database.
  
  ## Open questions / things skipped
  
  Nothing skipped. All three source files and the single test file were read
  in full. The `CHANGELOG.md`, `README.md`, `README.tpl`, and license files
  were noted but not relevant to security review.
  
  ## Draft review fields
  
  - rating: positive
  - thoroughness: medium
  - understanding: medium
